A||N||2||poupas||Fiero stories||||6||
Z||000000||poupas||06-04-2001||06:10 PM||||Hello to everyone. I am new to the forum and think it is absolutely incredible. I have been a Fiero lover for years. I went to a car show,in Ottawa, Canada, in 1986, I was 14 years old, and come home with a '86 GT poster. I was hooked since then.<BR>     I bought my first Fiero in '91. A '84 4cyl. It was all white and had been converted to a '85GT(still a 4). I loved that car but totalled it in'95(drove into a stone wall). I now drive a '86GT, and love it even more than my first.<BR>     Anyway, the first time I drove a Fiero I had to stop for gas, guess what, I could not find the fuel door release for the life of me. I asked the gas guy, he didnt know, and it probably took half an hour to find it. Also, right before that little incident, actually the first time I tried to take the keys out, I couldnt. I didnt realize it had that little doohickey key release mechanism. Talk about being a novice.<BR>   But I wasnt the only novice, one time I needed a quick repair, and brought my car to Canadian Tire, for everybody that does not know Canadian Tire, it is a large franchise that offers repairs to all types of cars, a type of car hardware store. Anyway, they fixed whatever was wrong and when they were bringing it out the guy told me that I had a brake problem. It was fine when I went in so I checked and sure enough they didnt know how to work the handbrake. Ask me if I ever brought my car back there.<BR>    And here is the cake, I had my car stored for winter, and in the spring I was starting it up, or trying to. I had power but it wouldnt turn over, so I broke out my Fiero manual and started going through the steps, 1-yes, I did that, 2-ya  did that, 3- oh my god I am so stupid. I drove a automatic wagon all winter, and guess what, I wasnt depressing the clutch. I felt like a moron.<BR>       Thanks for listening<P>||||reg||6
Z||000001||Urchin||06-04-2001||09:17 PM||||hehe.  I  had the same experience with the gas door release, I felt like an idiot.  That same car, I had stored at my friends place about three to rour hour drive from here.  It was there for two years.  They had told me they would store it inside.  When I finally had the means to go out to pick it up, I discovered it burried in snow past the deck lid! Fluids had to be put in after a few hours of digging it out. My friend and his dad start cracking jokes about how it will never start, and that the cars were all made in mexico and being cheap pieces of crap.   I popped a battery in it turned the key, saying "don't let me down".  Whooom!, started without missing a beat.  The never commented on the fact that it started without a problem.  Just figured I'd share one of my proud moments.  <BR>By the way, welcome to the forum.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature2_3.jpg">   <P>If you can read this, I'm parked.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SPDFREK||06-05-2001||02:01 AM||||Welcome to the forum!<BR> Dont fell bad, sometimes I still reach for the floorboard when I stop for gas. Some of the looks I get after doing that then reaching for the rear window! LOL<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig3.jpg"> <BR>'88 Fiero 4cycl Turbo,<BR>'88 Fiero GT,<BR>'72 Nova 383 stroker||||reg||1
